-Going back over the error propagation equation; Say f = uv; df/du = 1 df/dv = 1 --> sigma_f = sqrt(sig_u^2 + sig_v^2); this is called adding in quadrature
-This helps in determining and error ellipse; asteroid impact statistics sometimes includes Mars which is a very very big ellipse and likely wrong
-Say f = auv; you can think of constants with variables with extremely small uncertainties; df/du = av, df/dv = au --> sigma_f^2 = sigma_u^2a^2v^2 + sigma_v^2a^2u^2 --> divide both sides by f and auv respectively --> sigma_ f= f*sqrt((sigma_u^2/u^2) + (sigma_v^2/v^2))
-Say f = u^2; df/du = 2u; sigma_f = (2u)^2sigma_u^2
-This is not the same as f = a^2; sigma_f = sqrt(2)*a*sigma_a
-Know these formulas fundamentally, it is the difference between being right and wrong in a paper

-Back to 'Fitting_data_F25-classroom' 
-Side error is very easy to do, thats the good part, the bad  part is that if you have too many variables the entire analysis comes down to 1
-Typically, the best thing to do is to do both the Monte Carlo and Error Propagation analysis to be extra sure 
-Chi-squared test
-Moving on to least squares
-Consider a scatter plot with error bars, such as one for a linear fit; I'm not entirely sure why, but he drew one so it must be relevant
