-scipy.optimize.leastsq is good for analyzing data for chi^2; the stp by step for this process is as follows:

	>>>Evaluate the function
	>>>Compute chi^2 vs. data
	>>>Vary the parameters and repeat
	>>>It explores parameter space, looking for the minimum chi^2
	>>>It uses a function minimization routine
	>>>Calculates gradients in chi^2 and walks down them
	>>>Parameter errors are related to second derivatives near chi^2 minimum

-Chi^2 space, trying to find the bottom of curve with many dimensions
-Chi^2 = summation((x_1-mu)/sigma)^2; this is the goodness of fit criterion
-A typical value of chi^2 is N-m; when chi^2 ~ 1, we have a good fit and call this the Reduced Chi^2
-Lots of misses by many sigma means you underestimated your error bar size or your theory is wrong
-Student t-test
-Fitting models to data, Bevington Chpts. 6, 8, 10, 11
